from ../tooling/cli/Lsp import Lsp

export anchor PluginModules:
    description:
        A plugin can give the Piton module of a file that is not Piton, so
        that a .pi file imports it like any other module. The plugin lists
        the extensions of these files in its declaration, and its module hook
        turns the text of such a file into Piton source. The compiler knows
        only the extensions and calls the hook. All knowledge of the file
        format is in the plugin.

        ```piton
        \\\
        export piton-plugin EmulPlugin:
            name: emul
            entry: ./src/index.ts
            modules: [emul]
        \\\
        ```

        ```typescript
        \\\
        export default definePlugin({
          module(ctx, file) {
            return synthesize(file.text, file.path);
          },
        });
        \\\
        ```

    resolution:
        An import path that ends in one of the listed extensions names that
        file, and only that file: the compiler does not try .pi or an index.
        The path resolves as every other module path does: relative to the
        importing file, from the source root when it starts with /, or in an
        installed package. A path that names no file is an error. Without the
        extension, a path names a .pi module.

    hook:
        The hook receives the path of the file, relative to the project root
        when the file is in the project and else absolute, and its text. In
        the editor the text is the unsaved buffer. It returns the Piton
        source of the module, as a string or as an object with a source
        field. The compiler parses and resolves that source as it does every
        other module, so its imports, exports and anchors follow the usual
        rules, and a name it does not export is an error.

    when:
        The hook runs when the compiler loads such a file: in `piton
        compile`, `piton check`, `piton build` and every other command that
        compiles, and in the language server. The result is kept by path and
        text, so the hook runs again only when the text of the file changes.
        This is the one place where plugin code runs while compiling, and it
        never runs for a project that imports no such file. The hook has to
        be quick and change nothing. It may only do what the plugin declares,
        and it cannot compile a project that imports such a file itself.

    conflicts:
        An extension is given modules by one plugin. The first plugin in the
        list of plugins that lists it wins. pi can't be listed.

    failures:
        A hook that throws, or that returns no source, is an error on the
        file, which names the plugin. The module is then not loaded, and the
        import that names it reports nothing further.

    editor: The language server, described in @{Lsp}, installs the hooks when it loads the project configuration, and runs one when it loads such a file, from disk or from a buffer.
